   207	| KZG accumulation, two self slots plus inline processor work (Astra) | ESTIMATE 0.82-1.00M, k=20 (Astra) | as today per node; work per node limited to the rows left | same gadgets; the processor is new | Kept as the alternative (section 3.2 dissent 1). |
   208	| Split accumulation (BCLMS) | ESTIMATE 0.2-0.8M rows non-native (Astra) | - | no (`MP:plonk/traces.rs:14-18` is a comment) | Rejected for now: no cycle, so the deferred group work stays non-native; nothing to reuse. https://eprint.iacr.org/2020/1618 |
   209	| Folding (Nova, HyperNova, ProtoStar, ProtoGalaxy) | folding verifier is 1-3 scalar multiplications on a cycle (https://eprint.iacr.org/2023/1192); non-native here, ESTIMATE 10-40k rows | fast steps; needs a wrapper that proves the inner verification relation to the stock Midnight verifier | no; no BLS12-381 cycle | Not first: nothing to reuse, and a foreign PLONK proof is not a folding instance, so the folded step must contain the PLONK verifier. Kept as a fallback for the whole inner layer, not only for U (Astra's correction: multi-instance folding exists, https://eprint.iacr.org/2023/573, https://eprint.iacr.org/2023/1106, and the final relation need not be a KZG opening). |
   210	| Inner STARK or folding layer, outer KZG wrap | wrap ESTIMATE k=20-23 | 50-500 KiB inner proofs (Astra, ESTIMATE) | no | Not now. Astra holds it as the fallback if the KZG node fails fit or throughput (section 3.2 dissent 6). |
   211	| Per-level or per-shape keys | fits | - | yes | Rejected: GP-4 forbids per-depth keys. |
   212	| Unreleased upstream gadget cuts (5-15% fewer verifier rows) | helps every KZG option | changes keys and the proof format | no (`prover.md:123`) | Watch; not a plan input. |
   213	
   214	Lift sizes are hypotheses, not planning bounds (Astra's verdict "unsupported"; accepted). The
   215	only measured binary baseline is the 730,243-row probe, which has one shared name set, one
   216	collapse and no canonical disabled-slot construction. R = up to two leaf verifications with a
   217	constant key and one shared base resolution (the pattern of
   218	`crates/mori-recursion/src/inner.rs:273-314`): ESTIMATE 600-800k. F = one adapter-class
   219	verification, witnessed-key resolution of about 40 bases at 3-4k rows, a carried accumulator,
   220	two registry paths: ESTIMATE 550-750k. C = one verification plus three name sets: ESTIMATE
   221	700-950k. The decider cannot do C's work itself: two more name sets at 30-45 bases and 3-4k
   222	rows are 180-360k rows (ESTIMATE) against 119,410 rows of headroom (2^20 minus 929,166). The
   223	two-stage end (C then D) is therefore the expected case, and D must fold both C's own
   224	obligation and C's published accumulator (`inner.rs:182-207` is the pattern).
   225	
   226	Throughput (ESTIMATE). A run of M leaves needs about M/2 R proofs, M/2 - 1 merge nodes and one
   227	seal, about M node-class proofs, close to today's chain count; merges wait for their children,
   228	and the only contention figure on record is a 1.69-1.74 slowdown for three concurrent provers
   229	(`speed-levers.json:11-16`), so P workers give less than P. Final cost: one C proof and one D
   230	proof; their time is unmeasured.
   231	
   232	### 2.2 Q2 Universal VM and committed memory
   233	
   234	The leaf sees a fixed register file and commitments. Everything of unbounded size is behind a
   235	commitment that a step opens where it reads or writes.
   236	
   237	`sd = H(SD, control registers, outcome, fuel and counters, stack_h, arena_root, arena_len, env
   238	handle, deps_d)`. `prog_d = H(PROG, prog_root, machine version, rule inventory id)`.
   239	`input_d = H(IN, input value digest, fuel, stack limit)`. Initial fuel and the stack limit are
   240	semantic parameters of initialization (`crates/mori-core/src/sherlock/reference.rs:14`), so they
   241	are in `input_d`; a prover's memory capacity is not.
   242	
   243	| Structure | Commitment | Cost per access, as a formula (`c` = rows of one tagged tree compression with ordering and selection, at least 18 and unmeasured; both designers withdrew their round-1 figures, 18 and 50-150) |
   244	|---|---|---|
   245	| Program table (immutable) | Merkle root, depth 20 | read: d x c |
   246	| Frames (13 lanes each, `D/design.md` §2.1) | hash chain, top frames in registers | push, pop or rewrite of the top: about 7-8 absorption chunks (13 lanes plus the previous digest and a tag) |
   247	| Data arena (append-only, child before parent) | append-only Merkle tree, depth 32, with length | read: d x c; append or write: 2d x c |
   248	| Environment | persistent structure in the arena | as arena |
   249	| Input | Merkle or hash chain under `input_d` | as arena read |
   250	| Data-field references, environment cells, value slots (three more append tables, `crates/mori-core/src/sherlock/helper_machine/c6_phys_data.rs:11-18`) | one append-only tree each, or one tree with a table tag | as arena |
   251	| Helper task stack (7-lane entries, push, pop, rewrite of the top; `c6_phys_helper.rs:10-13`; `shared/owner_runtime.rs:3566-3577`) | hash chain | as frames |
   252	| Helper record (23 lanes) and result record (4) | registers | 0 |
   253	
   254	| Memory option | Composes in any PCD shape | Call |
   255	|---|---|---|
   256	| Merkle trees and hash chains | yes: a segment is provable from `pre_d` alone | Kept as the first implementation. Both designers. |
   257	| Paged Merkle (page-in, page-out per leaf; RISC Zero's continuations, https://dev.risczero.com/api/zkvm/optimization) | yes | Kept as the first optimisation, after locality is measured. |
   258	| Lookup or permutation memory with a challenge | yes, if every record is committed before the challenge that tests it: inside one proof, or over a sealed run or an epoch with authenticated boundaries (https://eprint.iacr.org/2025/105) | Kept as a throughput candidate, after Merkle. The round-1 Fable statement that it cannot compose was too strong (section 3.3). |
   259	| Multiset hash into a curve (https://arxiv.org/abs/1601.06502) | yes, additive and challenge-free; needs a final sweep and a linear-use discipline | Alternative if Merkle paths dominate (experiment 5). |
   260	| Whole-state hashing (today) | yes | Rejected: fact 5. |
   261	
   262	Depth is disputed (section 3.2 dissent 4).
   263	
   264	Outcomes: the terminal step writes `(outcome_kind, value_d)`; `value_d` digests the canonical
   265	value encoding. For a structured value this is a hash of the value itself, computed by a
   266	traversal, not an arena root and handle: two runs with different allocation histories must
   267	give the same `value_d` (Astra's correction; adopted). Owner-step height counts machine steps only; memory openings never consume a
   268	height (Astra's point; adopted).
   269	
   270	Genesis: with committed memory the initial state is a few constants plus `prog_root` and
   271	`input_d`, so the first leaf proves it in circuit for one state hash. Both designers now hold
   272	that in-circuit genesis is required: a claim consumed in circuit cannot rest on off-chain
   273	recomputation. This closes dissent 2 of
   274	`research/sherlock-recursion-design-review-20261110/review.md` 4.2.
   275	
   276	Risk: the fragments read lanes of the bounded image `sherlock-c6-phys/1` today. How much of
   277	each fragment survives depends on how many distinct cells a rule touches (experiment 5).
   278	
   279	### 2.3 Q3 Registry and adapters
   280	
   281	Key identity: `vk_id = H(VK, class_id, transcript_repr, comm_d)`, with `class_id = H(arch, k,
   282	exact public-input count, proof and key format version, transcript id, srs id)` and `comm_d` the Poseidon digest of the fixed and
   283	permutation commitments in key order. It is a deterministic function of the key bytes, so
   284	anyone can recompute it from a deployed Midnight key (`ZS:lib.rs:1537-1562` for the layout).
   285	The public-input count is in `MidnightVK` and not in `transcript_repr`, so it must be bound
   286	here (Astra's finding; verified). F does not recompute Blake2b; the binding of
   287	`transcript_repr` to `comm_d` is as strong as admission, so admission must check that the
   288	tuple is the normalization of real key bytes, by governance or by an admission proof.
   289	The SRS identity is part of the class because the pairing equation is specific to one SRS
   290	(`MP:poly/kzg/params.rs:235-237`; Astra's point, adopted): a key over another SRS cannot be
   291	accumulated with ours.
   292	
   293	Registry: Poseidon Merkle tree of `H(REG, vk_id, kind)`, kind in {direct, adapter, run};
   294	root in contract state; `admitKey` appends. A direct key is a foreign circuit: the claim about
   295	it has `id = vk_id`, which the adapter computes from the witnessed key and F checks against the
   296	registry. An adapter key may emit FOREIGN claims about direct keys. A run entry is a Sherlock
   297	leaf identity allowed to emit RUN claims: U at first; a different leaf prover later would be a
   298	second run entry and would enter through F. Adapter and run entries make claims on behalf of
   299	other identities and need governed admission; an adapter key can be checked by regenerating
   300	it from its class. Admitting an adapter does not admit the circuits it can verify: F also
   301	proves that the adapter's `vk_id_inner` is a registry member (round 1 omitted this; section
   302	3.3). Whether a proof made under an older `reg_root` stays usable after an admission is a
   303	policy choice: current root only, or an authenticated root history with a revocation rule.
   304	
   305	| Mechanism for a foreign circuit | Per-class key | Status | Call |
   306	|---|---|---|---|
   307	| Adapter circuit `A_c` using the existing gadget, generic over the verified class; all adapters share one adapter class, which F's verifier is built for | yes, registry data | gadget exists; the repository has verified ZKIR children in circuit (`qa/sherlock/manifests/c6-leaf.json` records a ZKIR leaf with a Poseidon transcript) | Recommended. |
   308	| Verifier as an authenticated program run by an in-node processor (Astra) | no | not built anywhere | Alternative; meets GP-3 without adapter keys. Cost unmeasured. |
   309	| Foreign key as a node constant | per key | exists (today's leaf) | Rejected: GP-3 forbids it. |
   310	| One slot per class in the node | no | - | Rejected: rows. |
   311	
   312	Compact contract proofs. Format (verified): proof tag `proof[v5]`, key tag `verifier-key[v7]`
   313	(`L:transient-crypto/src/proofs.rs:129-133,368-372`); the key is `MidnightVK` (arch, k,
   314	public-input count, PLONK key); Blake2b transcript; one public-input vector whose first
   315	elements are the binding input and the optional communications commitment, followed by the
   316	transcript of ledger operations (`L:zkir-v3/src/ir_vm.rs:213-217`; `L:ledger/src/verify.rs`
   317	`public_inputs_len`, "the binding input and the communication commitment"); trailing key bytes
   318	are refused (`L:transient-crypto/src/proofs.rs:528-535`). Consequences:
   319	
   320	- The adapter commits to the whole public-input vector (`out_d`) and interprets nothing. A
   321	  typed adapter that exposes a return value needs the opening of the communications commitment
   322	  as a witness (Astra's point; adopted).
   323	- A verified contract-circuit proof shows circuit satisfaction for those public inputs. It does
   324	  not show that a transaction was included on chain (Astra's point; adopted). The binding input
   325	  ties the proof to a call context; inclusion is the ledger's business.
   326	- The adapter only prepares the foreign proof. The proof is valid when the resulting
   327	  accumulator satisfies the pairing, so the adapter publishes that accumulator and F folds it
   328	  beside the adapter proof's own obligation (Astra's finding; round 1 omitted it).
   329	- The stdlib configuration has two instance columns (committed and ordinary) and the gadget
   330	  asserts one challenge phase; the ledger passes no committed instance, which the stdlib
   331	  replaces with the identity commitment (`L:transient-crypto/src/proofs.rs:563-575`;
   332	  `ZS:lib.rs:2020-2023`). Compact circuits use this configuration.
   333	- The public-input count is exact per adapter class (1.4 item 2). Zero-padding to a cap, as
   334	  round 1 proposed, changes the transcript and is wrong. A variable-length absorption would
   335	  need a changed verifier gadget. The ledger maximum is 65,536 inputs per call
   336	  (`L:onchain-runtime/src/transcript.rs:87`).
   337	- Guarded-off ledger operations stay in the vector as zero fields
   338	  (`L:zkir-v3/src/ir_vm.rs:507-523`); the adapter keeps positions as they are.
   339	
   340	### 2.4 Q4 PCD shape
   341	
   342	Node: two slots, each off or one proof under N, R or F. The all-off node is refused. R has a
   343	one-leaf mode for odd counts and single-leaf runs. A disabled
   344	slot contributes canonical neutral accumulator encodings to the fold (Astra's point; the
   345	current genesis handling at `ivc.rs:538-546` is the pattern, and it is why `collapse_self`
   346	cannot be removed as a local optimisation).
   347	
   348	Shape binding has two parts. `set_root` binds the claim DAG by content: `leaf(c) =
   349	H(LEAFCLAIM, c)`, `join = H(JOIN, l, r)`, `dep = H(DEP, c_run, deps_root)`. Content addressing
   350	identifies equal subgraphs, ignores unary nodes and ignores how a run's segments were
   351	bracketed; that is intended for claims and it is not the whole proof graph. `shape_d` binds
   352	the proof graph at every R, F and N. Both are in the proved statement, not in an off-chain
   353	manifest. Today it is
   354	the manifest: the Quint decider "does not read the manifest" and shape binding is "decider plus
   355	verify_chain" (`models/sherlock/quint/aggregation.qnt:16-22,464-467`). A SEG carries no shape by
   356	design: `link` is associative (`Aggregation.lean:291`) and the bracketing of one run does not
   357	change its statement (`same_leaves_same_stmt`, `:708`).
   358	
   359	DAG fan-out: the SET proof of claim A is the right child of the seal node of B and of the seal
   360	node of C. A is proved once and verified twice.
   361	
   362	| Object | Linear or reusable | Mechanism |
   363	|---|---|---|
   364	| Machine steps of one run | linear: each height exactly once | segment adjacency (`left.post = right.pre`, `left.hi = right.lo`); existing `Covers`, `linkAll_covers` (`Aggregation.lean:787,858`); Quint I3 (`aggregation.qnt:410`) |
   365	| Genesis | exactly one per `(prog_d, input_d)` | the seal rule requires `h_lo = 0` and the first leaf's `Init` check; replaces the decider check (`non_genesis_refused`, `Aggregation.lean:1106`; Quint I6) |
   366	| Claims | reusable facts | a claim may appear under several parents |
   367	| Settlement effects | linear | the contract's set of opened claims (a claim identifier is the nullifier). The set of accepted roots alone does not prevent replay: `JOIN(A, B)` and `JOIN(B, A)` are different roots containing A (Astra's finding) |
   368	
   369	Model changes needed: the Quint invariants I8 "program fixity" and I9 "no certificate is folded
   370	twice" (`aggregation.qnt:457-467`) forbid a mixed-program batch and a shared ancestor and must
   371	be replaced for the claim layer (Astra's finding; verified). Lean needs a claim layer above
   372	`Seg` (an inductive `SetCert` with seal, dep and join), the theorem "every claim under a
   373	decided `set_root` has a sound certificate", injectivity of `shape_d`, "no unfinished object is
   374	decided", an extended `result_unique` for the new outcomes and dependencies, and a
   375	well-founded extraction argument for reused certificates (no dependency-cycle forgery was
   376	found by either designer; none is proved absent).
   377	
   378	### 2.5 Q5 On-chain fit
   379	
   380	One contract for every program, `SherlockClaims`:
   381	
   382	- state: `reg_root`, `roots: Set<Field>`, `claims: Set<Field>`.
   383	- `acceptRoot(set_root, n, shape_d)`: verifier key = decider D, by the same mechanism as today (a
   384	  hand-written key behind a Compact transcript template,
   385	  `tools/sherlock-sdk/sherlock-run.compact:1-15`). The template reads `reg_root` from the
   386	  ledger, so a proof made against another registry is refused, as `acceptRun` does for
   387	  `program_d` (`:40-47`). A root already accepted is refused.
   388	- `openClaim(root, item, path)`: an ordinary Compact circuit with a fixed maximum path length
   389	  (for example 64); proves the path from a claim or from a sub-root to an accepted root. A
   390	  sub-root is added to `roots`, so a tree deeper than the maximum is opened in several calls
   391	  (Astra's finding: a Compact circuit cannot take a path of arbitrary depth). A claim is
   392	  inserted into `claims`, once per claim, so a shared ancestor has one effect. The claim tree
   393	  must use the hash Compact exposes (experiment 7).
   394	- `admitKey(vk_id, kind)`.
   395	
   396	| Limit | Value | Fit |
   397	|---|---|---|
   398	| Verifier key bytes | 50,000 (`L:transient-crypto/src/proofs.rs:422-431`) | decider key is 2,217 B (`docs/sherlock/stagenet-gp-recursion.md:198`) |
   399	| Public inputs per call | 65,536 (`L:onchain-runtime/src/transcript.rs:87`) | about 70 today; the new template is of the same order |
   400	| Operations per contract | 256 (`L:ledger/src/structure.rs:1928`) | three |
   401	| Decider k | no k check in `VerifierKey::verify` (`L:transient-crypto/src/proofs.rs:563-575`); a k=20 decider verifies (`speed-levers.json:95-109`) | D is expected to stay at k=20 because C does the base resolution (experiment 2) |
   402	
   403	Options: a single `acceptRun`-style entry with optional single-run fields (Astra) or
   404	`acceptRoot` plus `openClaim` (Fable). They are compatible: the single-run entry is a second
   405	template over the same decider that exposes one claim's fields.
   406	
   407	## 3. Agreement and dissent
   408	
   409	Process. Round 1: two proposals written without sight of each other (`fable/design-1.md`,
   410	`astra/design-1.md`). Round 2: Astra read both and wrote 26 findings against the Fable
   411	proposal, a table of the differences with its position on each, and its withdrawals (`astra/design-2.md`); Codex exit 0 in
   412	both rounds. The editor then re-read the sources for each finding used below. The first Astra
   413	run was started with a network flag that Codex ignored ("`sandbox_read_only` is ignored",
   414	`astra/design-1.log`); the lane still had Codex's own web search, and round 2 ran with
   415	`--search`. The URLs cited in this document were fetched for the editor and checked against
   416	their abstracts or page text, except https://eprint.iacr.org/2025/105, which is per Astra.
   417	
   418	### 3.1 Agreed
   419	
   420	1. **Primitive.** Keep midnight-proofs PLONK/KZG with the existing accumulation and a terminal
   421	   pairing decider. It is the only option with local implementation evidence. "Defer the
   422	   pairings" is already done; the cost is the collapse MSM.
   423	2. **Node.** One self-recursive node with two predecessor slots; no third proof slot at k=20.
   424	   The first thing to measure is the exact binary node with canonical disabled slots, because
   425	   the 730,243-row probe is incomplete (`crates/mori-recursion/tests/tree_node_rows.rs:1-20`).
   426	3. **Named core keys are sound in principle and need no fork.** Astra confirmed that N, R and
   427	   F can share a verifier slot when their full stdlib configuration matches, and showed a
   428	   public-API route: prepare the slot, take the accumulator's encoding, assign a replacement
   429	   accumulator over the union of the three name sets (`MC:verifier/accumulator.rs:280`;
   430	   `MC:verifier/verifier_gadget.rs:160`), and constrain each coefficient to `b_x * s_j`. The
   431	   conditions for soundness, which the library does not enforce: boolean selectors summing to
   432	   the slot bit; the same selectors choosing the representation, the coefficient destination
   433	   and the allowed statement type; one fixed coordinate order (names are not in the encoding);
   434	   children carrying the same three representations, pinned at the end; a child's inherited
   435	   accumulator keeping its names; canonical disabled encodings before the challenge; one SRS.
   436	   Astra revised its round-1 rule "carry only the self name set": a fixed finite union does not
   437	   grow with the registry. A security argument and adversarial proof tests are still owed.
   438	4. **For the named-lift design the end is two proofs**, a resolution proof then the pairing
   439	   decider (Astra: "a first-class candidate"; one-stage and two-stage are measured together).
   440	   Astra's own design ends in one decider, whose fit it puts at k=20-21, unmeasured.
   441	5. **Committed memory, Merkle first.** Whole-state hashing is replaced by commitments opened
   442	   per access. Frames and helper tasks as hash stacks (Astra adopted this from the Fable
   443	   proposal after checking that the machine rewrites the top,
   444	   `crates/mori-core/src/sherlock/helper_machine/shared/owner_runtime.rs:3566-3585`).
   445	6. **In-circuit genesis.** Off-chain recomputation of `init_d` cannot stand once claims are
   446	   consumed in circuit.
   447	7. **Claims are reusable; steps are linear.** The same proof may enter two parents. The Quint
   448	   invariants I8 and I9 must change. Settlement uniqueness is a separate, contract-level
   449	   property keyed by claim, not by root.
   450	8. **Registry.** A root in contract state, membership proved in circuit, identity binding the
   451	   full verification interpretation including the exact public-input count, the format
   452	   versions, the transcript and the SRS. Per-class adapter keys are registry data and do not
   453	   change the core keys (Astra withdrew its round-1 kill criterion on this point).
   454	9. **Compact proof facts.** Blake2b on the ledger, Poseidon in the gadget; the binding input,
   455	   the communications commitment, guarded-off zeros, the identity committed instance, complete
   456	   parsing. A verified circuit proof is circuit satisfaction, not inclusion.
   457	10. **Shape and claim content are separate commitments**, both in the statement.
   458	11. **One contract**, a decider key behind a Compact template, limits as in section 2.5; k=20
   459	    is a resource target, not a ledger maximum.
   460	12. **No concrete security bound** exists for unbounded-depth PCD with this construction; the
   461	    Lean accumulator law is a premise.
   462	13. **Hash costs are unmeasured.** Both round-1 figures are withdrawn.
   463	
   464	### 3.2 Dissent kept
   465	
   466	1. **Where new work enters the node.**
   467	   Fable: through proofs. The leaf U stays a separate proof and enters by the run lift R; the
   468	   node does only linking, sealing and hashing. Reasons: a leaf at k=20 has the whole domain
   469	   for VM work and a k=20 node has about 266k rows left after two self slots (ESTIMATE: 0.95 x
   470	   2^20 minus 730,243, before disabled-slot hardening), so inline work gives about half the VM
   471	   rows per node-class proof (ESTIMATE); the rule fragments and the leaf measurements now
   472	   running carry over; leaves prove in parallel.
   473	   Astra: inline. A bounded processor runs inside N beside the two self slots; core keys are N
   474	   and D only. Reasons: it meets "two prior proofs plus new work" in one node; fewer core keys;
   475	   long operations (memory paths, foreign verification, set union) become continuations
   476	   instead of new circuits. Astra's verdict on the Fable position: the two-node reading is a
   477	   narrowing and should not be put to the owner as an interpretation; named lifts are "a
   478	   useful optimization candidate".
   479	   Settled by: experiment 1 (rows of both nodes) and experiment 5 (VM rows per step). Astra's
   480	   kill criterion for the k=20 inline candidate is "no useful inline work"; the editor proposes
   481	   150k rows left as that threshold.
   482	2. **How a foreign verifier runs.**
   483	   Fable: adapter circuits per class, built from the existing gadget; a class outside the
   484	   stdlib configuration has no path until someone writes an adapter for it.
   485	   Astra: class adapters as fast paths, plus a general path: the verifier description is
   486	   authenticated data executed by the processor across nodes, so no new key is ever needed for
   487	   a new architecture.
   488	   Editor's note: the general path is a PLONK verifier interpreter with non-native MSM as its
   489	   inner loop; at 3-4k rows per base it is several nodes per foreign proof even with a native
   490	   scalar-multiplication instruction (ESTIMATE), and nothing like it exists in the pinned
   491	   crates. Astra agrees it is "a proposed construction, not an implemented feasibility result".
   492	   Settled by: experiment 4, and by the owner's reading of "other PLONK/halo2 circuits".
   493	3. **Whether Poseidon re-proving counts for GP-3.**
   494	   Fable: a Poseidon-transcript proof under the deployed key proves the same statement and
   495	   satisfies the acceptance sentence "a proof from a Compact contract circuit"; ledger bytes
   496	   are the second stage.
   497	   Astra: it is an integration test. GP-3 is complete only when ledger proof bytes are
   498	   verified under their native transcript; re-proving needs the witness and verifies nothing
   499	   that was supplied.
   500	   Not resolved; owner question 2.
   501	4. **Fixed tree depth.**
   502	   Fable: a depth equal to the address width (32 or 64 bits) is not a capacity ladder; GP-1
   503	   itself bounds the height at 2^64.
   504	   Astra: any depth fixed by the relation is capacity bound to the key; storage must be
   505	   expandable, with path processing across proofs. The round-1 Fable depths (20 and 32) are a
   506	   narrowing; the editor accepts that for 20 and 32 and holds the position for the address
   507	   width.
   508	   Not resolved; owner question 3. It changes the leaf: a 64-level path costs twice a 32-level
   509	   one, and expandable storage needs continuations.
   510	5. **Claim collection.**
   511	   Fable: `set_root` is the content hash of the claim DAG; duplicates stay as occurrences;
   512	   uniqueness is enforced where effects happen (`openClaim`). No in-circuit set union.
   513	   Astra: a canonical map keyed by semantic claim identity, with insertion proved in circuit
   514	   as a streamed computation, so that the statement is a duplicate-free set and `n_claims`
   515	   counts distinct claims; plus a run identity that separates request context from the
   516	   computation.
   517	   Settled by: experiment 6 (cost of the union) and the owner's reading of "commits to the set
   518	   of claims".
   519	6. **Scope of the alternative proof systems.**
   520	   Fable: folding or a STARK is a possible later prover for the leaf, admitted as a registry
   521	   key that emits RUN claims; the node stays KZG because foreign PLONK proofs must be verified
   522	   in a PLONK circuit anyway.
   523	   Astra: a recursive STARK or multi-folding system is the fallback for the whole inner layer
   524	   if the KZG node fails fit or throughput.
   525	   Both: not first. Settled by experiment 8 only if experiments 1, 2 and 5 fail.
