   214	Lift sizes are hypotheses, not planning bounds (Astra's verdict "unsupported"; accepted). The
   215	only measured binary baseline is the 730,243-row probe, which has one shared name set, one
   216	collapse and no canonical disabled-slot construction. R = up to two leaf verifications with a
   217	constant key and one shared base resolution (the pattern of
   218	`crates/mori-recursion/src/inner.rs:273-314`): ESTIMATE 600-800k. F = one adapter-class
   219	verification, witnessed-key resolution of about 40 bases at 3-4k rows, a carried accumulator,
   220	two registry paths: ESTIMATE 550-750k. C = one verification plus three name sets: ESTIMATE
   221	700-950k. The decider cannot do C's work itself: two more name sets at 30-45 bases and 3-4k
   222	rows are 180-360k rows (ESTIMATE) against 119,410 rows of headroom (2^20 minus 929,166). The
   223	two-stage end (C then D) is therefore the expected case, and D must fold both C's own
   224	obligation and C's published accumulator (`inner.rs:182-207` is the pattern).
   225	
   226	Throughput (ESTIMATE). A run of M leaves needs about M/2 R proofs, M/2 - 1 merge nodes and one
   227	seal, about M node-class proofs, close to today's chain count; merges wait for their children,
   228	and the only contention figure on record is a 1.69-1.74 slowdown for three concurrent provers
   229	(`speed-levers.json:11-16`), so P workers give less than P. Final cost: one C proof and one D
   230	proof; their time is unmeasured.
