   312	Compact contract proofs. Format (verified): proof tag `proof[v5]`, key tag `verifier-key[v7]`
   313	(`L:transient-crypto/src/proofs.rs:129-133,368-372`); the key is `MidnightVK` (arch, k,
   314	public-input count, PLONK key); Blake2b transcript; one public-input vector whose first
   315	elements are the binding input and the optional communications commitment, followed by the
   316	transcript of ledger operations (`L:zkir-v3/src/ir_vm.rs:213-217`; `L:ledger/src/verify.rs`
   317	`public_inputs_len`, "the binding input and the communication commitment"); trailing key bytes
   318	are refused (`L:transient-crypto/src/proofs.rs:528-535`). Consequences:
   319	
   320	- The adapter commits to the whole public-input vector (`out_d`) and interprets nothing. A
   321	  typed adapter that exposes a return value needs the opening of the communications commitment
   322	  as a witness (Astra's point; adopted).
   323	- A verified contract-circuit proof shows circuit satisfaction for those public inputs. It does
   324	  not show that a transaction was included on chain (Astra's point; adopted). The binding input
   325	  ties the proof to a call context; inclusion is the ledger's business.
   326	- The adapter only prepares the foreign proof. The proof is valid when the resulting
   327	  accumulator satisfies the pairing, so the adapter publishes that accumulator and F folds it
   328	  beside the adapter proof's own obligation (Astra's finding; round 1 omitted it).
   329	- The stdlib configuration has two instance columns (committed and ordinary) and the gadget
   330	  asserts one challenge phase; the ledger passes no committed instance, which the stdlib
   331	  replaces with the identity commitment (`L:transient-crypto/src/proofs.rs:563-575`;
   332	  `ZS:lib.rs:2020-2023`). Compact circuits use this configuration.
   333	- The public-input count is exact per adapter class (1.4 item 2). Zero-padding to a cap, as
   334	  round 1 proposed, changes the transcript and is wrong. A variable-length absorption would
   335	  need a changed verifier gadget. The ledger maximum is 65,536 inputs per call
   336	  (`L:onchain-runtime/src/transcript.rs:87`).
   337	- Guarded-off ledger operations stay in the vector as zero fields
   338	  (`L:zkir-v3/src/ir_vm.rs:507-523`); the adapter keeps positions as they are.
   339	
   340	### 2.4 Q4 PCD shape
   341	
   342	Node: two slots, each off or one proof under N, R or F. The all-off node is refused. R has a
   343	one-leaf mode for odd counts and single-leaf runs. A disabled
   344	slot contributes canonical neutral accumulator encodings to the fold (Astra's point; the
   345	current genesis handling at `ivc.rs:538-546` is the pattern, and it is why `collapse_self`
   346	cannot be removed as a local optimisation).
   347	
   348	Shape binding has two parts. `set_root` binds the claim DAG by content: `leaf(c) =
   349	H(LEAFCLAIM, c)`, `join = H(JOIN, l, r)`, `dep = H(DEP, c_run, deps_root)`. Content addressing
   350	identifies equal subgraphs, ignores unary nodes and ignores how a run's segments were
   351	bracketed; that is intended for claims and it is not the whole proof graph. `shape_d` binds
   352	the proof graph at every R, F and N. Both are in the proved statement, not in an off-chain
   353	manifest. Today it is
   354	the manifest: the Quint decider "does not read the manifest" and shape binding is "decider plus
   355	verify_chain" (`models/sherlock/quint/aggregation.qnt:16-22,464-467`). A SEG carries no shape by
   356	design: `link` is associative (`Aggregation.lean:291`) and the bracketing of one run does not
   357	change its statement (`same_leaves_same_stmt`, `:708`).
   358	
   359	DAG fan-out: the SET proof of claim A is the right child of the seal node of B and of the seal
   360	node of C. A is proved once and verified twice.
   361	
   362	| Object | Linear or reusable | Mechanism |
   363	|---|---|---|
   364	| Machine steps of one run | linear: each height exactly once | segment adjacency (`left.post = right.pre`, `left.hi = right.lo`); existing `Covers`, `linkAll_covers` (`Aggregation.lean:787,858`); Quint I3 (`aggregation.qnt:410`) |
   365	| Genesis | exactly one per `(prog_d, input_d)` | the seal rule requires `h_lo = 0` and the first leaf's `Init` check; replaces the decider check (`non_genesis_refused`, `Aggregation.lean:1106`; Quint I6) |
   366	| Claims | reusable facts | a claim may appear under several parents |
   367	| Settlement effects | linear | the contract's set of opened claims (a claim identifier is the nullifier). The set of accepted roots alone does not prevent replay: `JOIN(A, B)` and `JOIN(B, A)` are different roots containing A (Astra's finding) |
   368	
   369	Model changes needed: the Quint invariants I8 "program fixity" and I9 "no certificate is folded
   370	twice" (`aggregation.qnt:457-467`) forbid a mixed-program batch and a shared ancestor and must
   371	be replaced for the claim layer (Astra's finding; verified). Lean needs a claim layer above
   372	`Seg` (an inductive `SetCert` with seal, dep and join), the theorem "every claim under a
   373	decided `set_root` has a sound certificate", injectivity of `shape_d`, "no unfinished object is
   374	decided", an extended `result_unique` for the new outcomes and dependencies, and a
   375	well-founded extraction argument for reused certificates (no dependency-cycle forgery was
   376	found by either designer; none is proved absent).
   377	
   378	### 2.5 Q5 On-chain fit
   379	
   380	One contract for every program, `SherlockClaims`:
   381	
   382	- state: `reg_root`, `roots: Set<Field>`, `claims: Set<Field>`.
   383	- `acceptRoot(set_root, n, shape_d)`: verifier key = decider D, by the same mechanism as today (a
   384	  hand-written key behind a Compact transcript template,
   385	  `tools/sherlock-sdk/sherlock-run.compact:1-15`). The template reads `reg_root` from the
   386	  ledger, so a proof made against another registry is refused, as `acceptRun` does for
   387	  `program_d` (`:40-47`). A root already accepted is refused.
   388	- `openClaim(root, item, path)`: an ordinary Compact circuit with a fixed maximum path length
   389	  (for example 64); proves the path from a claim or from a sub-root to an accepted root. A
   390	  sub-root is added to `roots`, so a tree deeper than the maximum is opened in several calls
   391	  (Astra's finding: a Compact circuit cannot take a path of arbitrary depth). A claim is
   392	  inserted into `claims`, once per claim, so a shared ancestor has one effect. The claim tree
   393	  must use the hash Compact exposes (experiment 7).
   394	- `admitKey(vk_id, kind)`.
   395	
   396	| Limit | Value | Fit |
   397	|---|---|---|
   398	| Verifier key bytes | 50,000 (`L:transient-crypto/src/proofs.rs:422-431`) | decider key is 2,217 B (`docs/sherlock/stagenet-gp-recursion.md:198`) |
   399	| Public inputs per call | 65,536 (`L:onchain-runtime/src/transcript.rs:87`) | about 70 today; the new template is of the same order |
   400	| Operations per contract | 256 (`L:ledger/src/structure.rs:1928`) | three |
   401	| Decider k | no k check in `VerifierKey::verify` (`L:transient-crypto/src/proofs.rs:563-575`); a k=20 decider verifies (`speed-levers.json:95-109`) | D is expected to stay at k=20 because C does the base resolution (experiment 2) |
   402	
   403	Options: a single `acceptRun`-style entry with optional single-run fields (Astra) or
   404	`acceptRoot` plus `openClaim` (Fable). They are compatible: the single-run entry is a second
   405	template over the same decider that exposes one claim's fields.
   406	
   407	## 3. Agreement and dissent
   408	
   409	Process. Round 1: two proposals written without sight of each other (`fable/design-1.md`,
   410	`astra/design-1.md`). Round 2: Astra read both and wrote 26 findings against the Fable
